{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וגלחה את ראשה", :translit "v'gelachah et roshah", :gloss "and she shall shave her head"} {:he "לפי שהיטיבה ראשה לפתותך", :translit "l'fi sheheitivah roshah l'fotatkha", :gloss "because she beautified her head to seduce you"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Motif of enemy women using beauty to entice Israel into sin; the shaving counters the seductive adornment"}], :frame {:text "Deuteronomy 21:12 prescribes that a beautiful war captive shave her head before being taken as a wife. The frame reads this act as neutralizing the very beauty that was deployed as a weapon. The paradigmatic case is the Midianite woman Cozbi, whose name and deeds are recorded at Numbers 25:15; Psalm 106:28-31 describes how Israel's entanglement with Midianite women provoked zeal and was counted as a covenant act. The rabbis in Sotah 11b trace the strategic use of female beauty to entice Israel back to Pharaoh's decree in Exodus 1:22.", :sources ["Numbers 25:15" "Psalm 106:28-31" "Sotah 11b" "Exodus 1:22"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ועשתה את צפרניה", :translit "v'asatah et tzaporneyha", :gloss "and she shall do her nails"} {:he "לפי שרמזה לך בהם לפתותך", :translit "l'fi she-ramzah l'cha bahem l'fotatkha", :gloss "because she hinted to you with them to seduce you"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: רמזה (hinted/gestured) echoes צפרניה (nails) — nails used as instruments of beckoning"}], :frame {:text "Deuteronomy 21:12 pairs the shaving of the captive woman's head with the trimming of her nails. The rabbis in Yevamot 48b derive from the parallelism of 'and she shall shave' and 'and she shall do' that both acts serve the same purpose of diminishing her beauty. Rashi on Deuteronomy 21:12, citing the Talmud, explains that she made herself repulsive so that he would not wish to marry her. The Targum Pseudo-Jonathan on Deuteronomy 21:12 expands that she grew her nails long for adornment.", :sources ["Yevamot 48b" "Rashi on Deuteronomy 21:12" "Targum Pseudo-Jonathan on Deuteronomy 21:12"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Motif of enemy women using beauty to entice Israel into sin; the shaving counters the seductive adornment", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: רמזה (hinted/gestured) echoes צפרניה (nails) — nails used as instruments of beckoning", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:37:45.124610184Z", :model "glm-5.2"}